# Phase 6 spec: society (minds that know each other and work together)

> **APPROVED 2026-09-26 (session 11). Build steps 1-5 BUILT (0 errors) and checked in the game; build step 6 (the
> society run) started, stopped at day 2.7 when the model was unloaded.** Results so far: §16. Written from the session 11
> discussion: the cooperation run on a fresh colony (Bernard, Lyra, Toni) and the user's decisions listed in §14.
> The user reviews this spec before any building starts.

**Goal (VISION.md roadmap, Phase 6):** several AI minds that talk to each other, know what the others are doing, and
split up the colony's work between them. The test is the roadmap's: *three minds hold a conversation and split up the
chores.* Phase 5 gave every mind the player's colony abilities (fields, stockpiles, hunting, cutting, mining, work
orders); Phase 6 makes them use those abilities **as a group**.

**Why now (session 11 findings):**
- With the firm cooperation rule in `system.txt`, the minds are friendly ("we need", "our supplies"), but each
  decides alone. Toni promised the player "rooms for everyone" and couldn't do it (a bedroom is always the designer's
  own); Bernard and Lyra slept in Toni's bed instead of building; three days of "Need meal source" and nobody planned a
  kitchen, each assuming someone else would.
- Two minds picked the same chore at the same moment, and the second one lost its decision ("Someone already marked
  them."). Nothing tells a mind what the others are working on.
- Replies wait behind each other (one request in flight); 13 of about 40 Act replies were dropped as stale in
  session 9 with three minds.
- The context has grown over five phases and is hard to follow. At midnight a mind loses its whole day
  (`[Today so far]` starts empty), and `[My plan]` and `[Recent]` don't say which day they're from.

**Scope decisions (the user, session 11):**
- **Clean up the context first**: `[My plan]` and `[Recent]` say *yesterday* or *today*; the special
  `[I promised the player]` section goes; talking with the player is ordinary conversation.
- **Minds knowing what the others do belongs here** (society), not in Phase 5. The user liked `[Others]`.
- A storyboard for how collaboration and conversations should work (§3), then the mechanics.
- Carries on from Phase 5's principles: world state from the map, the same for every mind; plain words, no coordinates;
  she chooses, code works it out; start light, tighten on evidence; let vanilla handle it.

**Builds on:** Phase 2 (talk lines, Chat), Phase 3 (events, person files, Reflect, `told` sources), Phase 4 (the Project
call, room kinds), Phase 5 (the Colony call, `ChoreManager`). No new dependencies.

**Out of scope:** a colony-level council or shared plan (Phase 7); new vanilla interaction defs; minds giving each
other orders (asking is fine, the other may say no); trading, diplomacy, raids and defence.

**Phase 5 still open:** its self-sufficiency run (build step 7) and closing checks. Build step 1 here (the context
clean-up) touches every call and can go first; the rest waits until Phase 5 is closed. See §13.

---

## 1. What a mind sees today (the pipeline, as built through Phase 5)
This is the reference the user asked for. Code: `PromptBuilder.cs` (recipes and order), `SnapshotBuilder.cs` (the
world), `Recall.cs` / `Retrieval.cs` (memory), `Prompts/*.txt` (the task text).

**The calls:**

| Call | When | What it decides | Template |
|---|---|---|---|
| Persona | once, when she gets a mind | the identity anchor + a first line | `persona.txt` |
| Plan | each morning; "re-plan" from the Act | today's intent, 24-hour schedule, work priorities | `plan.txt` |
| Act | wake triggers (idle, a few hours passed, events), up to 10 a day | one numbered option + a spoken line | `act.txt` |
| Chat | the player sends a message | a reply, an optional Act option, a promise note | `chat.txt` |
| Project | after "plan a new room" (Act or Chat) | room kind, size, site, material | `project.txt` |
| Colony | after "work on the colony" (Act or Chat) | one chore with its amount, crop, site, what it holds | `colony.txt` |
| Reflect | once a night, asleep | diary, lately, memories, person files, goals | `reflect.txt` |

**Every call = system prompt + user prompt.**
- **System prompt** (stable, cached by LM Studio): who she is (`system.txt`), her persona, the rules of thumb
  (`guidance.txt`), then `[Who I am lately]` and `[My goals]` from Reflect.
- **User prompt**: the task text with `{snapshot}` filled in. The snapshot is the recipe's sections, in this fixed
  order:

| Section | Source | Plan | Act | Chat | Project/Colony | Reflect |
|---|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|
| `[Me] [Time]` | the pawn, the clock | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `[Condition] [Needs] [Feelings] [Skills] [Doing now]` | the pawn | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | |
| `[My plan]` | this morning's (or yesterday's) intent | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | |
| `[I promised the player]` | Chat `note` → a "promise" goal | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | |
| `[My project]` | `BuildManager` | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | |
| `[People nearby]` | the map: relation, opinion, mood, distance | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | |
| `[Colony] [Colony stores] [Colony work] [Rooms]` | the map (the same for every mind) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | |
| `[Recent]` | her last 5 decisions (no day) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | |
| `[Today so far]` | memory events since midnight, top by importance | 5 | 3 | 5 (no chat) | 5 | |
| `[About X]` | her person files | people in today's events | up to 3 present | the player + anyone named | | |
| `[On my mind]` | ≤ 2 strong memory matches, ≤ once per 4 h | | ✓ | | | |
| `[I remember]` | ≤ 5 memories matched to the player's words | | | ✓ | | |

Reflect gets its own values instead (the day's events, the people, goals, the nearest memories).

**Measured in session 11** (50 Act calls): `[Today so far]` in 47, `[About X]` in 27, `[On my mind]` in 11. So day to
day, long-term memory reaches decisions mostly through `[Who I am lately]`, `[My goals]` and the person files.

## 2. What changes in the context (build steps 1-2)

### 2.1 Chronology: yesterday and today (build step 1)
Every time-stamped line says which day it's from. No new sections; the existing ones get day labels.
- **`[My plan]`**: `Today's plan (made at 07:00): Build the kitchen with Lyra.` Before today's Plan runs:
  `Yesterday's plan (not planned today yet): Build shelter walls.` This is the backlog item where yesterday's plan
  anchored the new one (Lover, session 9; Toni, session 11).
- **`[Recent]`** (her own decisions): grouped by day, oldest first:
  `Yesterday: 13:00 Laid out a potato field. · 21:00 Kept going. — Today: 07:00 Planned the day.` Still her last 5.
- **`[Today so far]` becomes `[Since yesterday]`**: the last 24 hours, not since midnight, so a mind thinking at 00:16
  still sees the evening before. Same caps (Act 3, others 5), each line with its day:
  `Yesterday 18:10 Deep talk with Lyra … · Today 06:40 Ate without table (mood -3)`. Reflect is unchanged (it still
  covers the events since the last Reflect).

### 2.2 The player is conversation, not a promise list (build step 1)
- **Removed:** `[I promised the player]`, the Chat `note` field, and `Goal.Promise` (the "promised the player" goals and
  their morning expiry). Old saves: promise goals are dropped on load.
- **What carries the conversation instead** (all of it exists already):
  - the same day: the chat lines are memory events ("The player said: …", "I told the player: …"), so they show in
    `[Since yesterday]` for every call;
  - the Chat call keeps its own `[Conversation with the player so far]` (8 turns, forgotten after 6 h of silence);
  - that night Reflect writes what mattered into the player's person file (impression and "threads": open matters
    between us, e.g. "said I'd plan a kitchen") and into memories.
- **One addition, so yesterday's conversation still reaches the day:** the morning Plan gets `[About the player]` when
  the player's file has open threads. (Today the Plan only gets files for people in today's events.)
- The chat prompt keeps "if you agree to do something right now, pick the option"; "later" is just what she said.

### 2.3 `[Others]`: what everyone else is doing (build step 2)
One short line per other colonist, for every call, placed after `[People nearby]`. It's world state (the same facts
for every mind), read fresh on every call:
> `[Others] Lyra (cook, builder): today: build a kitchen and beds · her kitchen: walls 12/20, waiting on 40 wood ·
> 2 h ago marked 10 trees to cut. — Bernard (miner): today: mine steel for the walls · 5 h ago marked steel to mine.
> — Snowberry (animal).`

- **Role words:** her two best skills with a passion, as plain nouns (miner, cook, builder, grower, doctor, hunter,
  crafter, artist, researcher, talker…). From `pawn.skills`; one table of words, no grammar code.
- **For a mind:** its intent for today (or "no plan yet today"), its active project line (`BuildProject`, as in
  `[My project]` but in the third person by name), and its last chore from `ChoreManager` with how long ago.
- **For a colonist without a mind:** name and role words only. Animals and prisoners aren't listed.
- **Cap:** 6 people, about 500 characters, closest first; "and 3 more" after that.
- Owned rooms are already in `[Rooms]` ("Lyra's bedroom"); `[Others]` doesn't repeat them.

## 3. Storyboard: three minds, one colony
The cast follows VISION.md: Mara (a medic, cooks well), Bo (a miner, blunt), Kira (a builder, timid). All three have
minds. Scenes 23-31 continue the VISION.md numbering. Each "under the hood" says which section of this spec does it.

### Scene 23: Everyone sees everyone
Morning. Mara plans her day. Her prompt says Bo is mining steel, Kira has a kitchen half built and is waiting on 40
wood. Mara plans "cook for everyone, and help Kira get her wood". She doesn't also start a kitchen.

> Under the hood: `[Others]` (§2.3) and `Today's plan` (§2.1). Nobody tells her to help; seeing the stall is enough.

### Scene 24: Asking for help
Kira's kitchen has waited on wood since yesterday. Kira picks "talk to Bo: chitchat" and says: *"Bo, could you cut some
trees for the kitchen? I'm stuck on wood."* Bo answers in his own voice: *"Fine. After this vein."* His reply picks
"work on the colony", and his Colony call marks 25 trees.

> Under the hood: the **Reply** (§4). When a mind talks to another mind, the other mind answers with one line and may
> act on it from its own menu, exactly like a reply to the player. Asking is ordinary conversation; there's no
> "request" system.

### Scene 25: Saying no, kindly
Mara asks Bo to take a shift at the stove. Bo is exhausted: *"Not now, I'm dead on my feet. Tomorrow."* Both remember
it. Next morning Bo's Plan sees `Yesterday 20:10 Mara asked me to cook; I said tomorrow.` and turns Cooking up.

> Under the hood: §4's reply with no action, `[Since yesterday]` (§2.1), then Reflect puts it in the files
> ("threads"). Minds may refuse each other, as they may refuse the player; the cooperation rule makes refusals polite
> and usually "later".

### Scene 26: No double work
Mara opens the Colony call. `[Others]` says Bo marked 25 trees an hour ago, so she sets up a rice field instead. At
the same moment Kira also picks "cut trees": code gives her the next 25 unmarked trees instead of failing.

> Under the hood: `[Others]` (§2.3) and the collision rule (§5.1).

### Scene 27: A room for someone else
Kira sleeps on the floor and her mood is sinking. Mara has a bed. Mara plans "a bedroom for Kira". When the bed is
built it's Kira's. Kira: *"You built this for me? Thank you."* Both remember it; Kira's opinion of Mara rises.

> Under the hood: the Project call's "for whom" (§5.2). Barracks stay the "beds for everyone" choice.

### Scene 28: The player's word is conversation
The player tells Mara: *"We really need a kitchen."* Mara: *"You're right. Kira's on it; I'll help her with the
stove."* There's no promise list. The same day her Act prompts show the exchange in `[Since yesterday]`; the next
morning her Plan shows `[About the player]` with the thread "asked for a kitchen; I said I'd help Kira".

> Under the hood: §2.2.

### Scene 29: Midnight doesn't erase the day
At 00:30 Bo wakes cold. His prompt still shows yesterday evening: the talk with Mara, the trees he marked. He doesn't
re-plan yesterday's work as if it hadn't happened.

> Under the hood: `[Since yesterday]` and the day labels in `[Recent]` (§2.1).

### Scene 30: Word gets around
Kira tells Mara over dinner: *"Bo was up all night in the mine."* Mara hasn't seen it. Her memory keeps it as
*Kira told me*, not as a fact. When she next sees Bo, she asks how he's holding up.

> Under the hood: the talk line is already a memory event for the listener; §4 tags it with source `told` and the
> speaker, so Reflect treats it as a claim (VISION.md Scene 11, Scene 16).

### Scene 31: The colony splits the work (the Phase 6 goal)
A fresh colony, three minds, no player input. By day 3 each has a role they talk about ("I'll farm, you mine"), the
work is split with no double marks, everyone has a bed, and a kitchen is built. The social log shows real exchanges:
a line and an answer.

> Under the hood: all of the above. Check 8 (§11).

## 4. The Reply: one mind answers another (build step 3)
Today a talk between two minds carries only the first speaker's line; the listener gets a memory event and says
nothing. Phase 6 gives the listener one answer.

**When:** a mind's talk (its Act chose "talk to X: …", with a line) lands on a colonist **who also has a mind**, awake
and not in a mental break. Vanilla random chitchat stays canned (no call). One Reply per talk; the first speaker
doesn't answer back (two lines is a conversation; a third is Phase 7 cost).

**The call** (`reply`, template `Prompts/reply.txt`), from the listener's side. It is the Chat call with another
colonist in the player's place:
- recipe: the listener's full snapshot + `[Since yesterday]` + `[About <speaker>]` + `[I remember]` (up to 3 memories
  matched to the line, as Chat does);
- the task: *"<speaker> just said to you: '<line>' (<interaction label>). Answer out loud, in character, 1-2 short
  sentences. If they ask for something and you agree to do it now, pick the option that does it; otherwise 0."*
- the same menu as Chat (the Act menu), `0: nothing right now` first.

```json
{ "reply": "Fine. After this vein.", "act": 17, "memory": 0 }
```

**Applies via:** the reply is shown as a solo speech entry from the listener right after the talk (a bubble and the
social log, `SpeechLog.Say`), with the same save-safety as today's remarks. `act` > 0 runs the option like a Chat
`act` (Project and Colony calls included; it counts as the listener's decision, not a free one). Both minds record the
exchange: the speaker "Bo answered: '…'" (source `took_part`), the listener "Kira asked me …; I said …".

**Budget and guards:** a Reply costs the listener one decision from its daily budget; if it has none left, it still
answers but `act` is not offered. It's dropped if the listener is drafted, downed, asleep or in a break by the time it
lands (stale rule, as for Act). Negative interactions (insult, slight) get a reply too, and it may be sharp; the
cooperation rule still holds.

**Gossip:** a listener's memory event for a talk line gets source `told` and the speaker's name (today it's
`took_part`), so Reflect treats what was said as a claim (Scene 30).

## 5. Working together on the map (build step 4)

### 5.1 No double work
- **Collision rule:** when a Hunt / Cut / Gather / Mine option is applied and some of its targets were marked by
  someone else meanwhile, the finder runs again at apply time and takes the next free targets of the same kind, up
  to the chosen count. Only if none are left does it fail ("Someone already marked the rest."). Replaces today's
  "Someone already marked them." after a stale menu.
- `[Others]` shows each mind's last chore, so most doubles never happen.
- No reservations or locks between minds; the Phase 5 rule stands: each mind only stops what it set up.

### 5.2 Rooms for someone else
- The Project call gets a **"for"** field when the chosen kind is a bedroom: `me`, or the name of a colonist with no
  bed of their own (enum; only offered if there is one). Code claims the finished bed for that person instead of the
  designer. `[My project]` says "a bedroom for Kira"; `[Others]` shows it on Mara's line; Kira gets a memory when it's
  done ("Mara built me a bedroom", importance 6).
- The designer still owns the project (cancel, abandon, one at a time). The occupant can't abandon it.
- Barracks stay as they are (unowned beds for anyone).

## 6. The request queue (build step 5)
Several minds share one LM Studio. Today `LlmClient` runs one request at a time, first come first served, and a reply
older than an hour of game time is dropped.
- **Priority queue:** player Chat > Reply > Act > Project/Colony > Plan > Reflect. A waiting Reflect never delays a
  conversation (Phase 3's cancel-and-rerun stays).
- **Parallel slots:** a setting "Parallel requests" (default 1, up to 4) for servers that run several at once
  (LM Studio's parallel option). Measure stale drops and seconds per call at 1 and at 2 before changing the default.
- Stale rule unchanged; log each drop with how long it waited in the queue, so the cost of waiting is visible.

## 7. What stays the same
- Every mind gets the same world facts; `[Others]` is world state, not memory.
- Minds may refuse the player and each other. The cooperation rule (session 11, `system.txt`) makes them helpful and
  civil; traits change tone, not willingness.
- Vanilla does the work; minds only decide, talk and set things up.
- No coordinates or directions in prompts (`no_coords.py`).

## 8. Guardrails
- A Reply is only between two minds, only for a talk a mind started, and never chains (no reply to a reply).
- Daily decision budget applies to Replies that act (§4).
- "for" in the Project call only names colonists who have no bed of their own, checked again when the bed is claimed
  (if they've got one meanwhile, the bed stays unclaimed and it's an ordinary room).
- `[Others]` never shows another mind's reasons, memories or diary: only what anyone in the colony could see or be
  told (their plan for the day is said at breakfast, in effect).

## 9. Settings and dev tools
- Settings: "Minds answer each other" (on), "Parallel requests" (1).
- Debug actions (`Actions\…`, runnable through RimBridge):
  - "Show context now" (selected mind): writes the full Act prompt it would get to
    `D:\RimDev\AIPawnControl\context.txt`, with each section's character count. The pipeline table in §1, live.
  - "Talk to <mind> now…" (children per other mind): the selected mind starts a deep talk with a fixed test line, so
    the Reply runs without waiting for an Act.
  - "Queue status": requests waiting, by kind, and how long.

## 10. Save data and logging
- No new saved classes. `Goal.Promise` is dropped on load. The Reply's lines are solo entries (left out of saves like
  every remark). The "for" of a bedroom is saved on `BuildProject` (`occupant`).
- JSONL: a new call type `reply`. Player.log: `<listener> reply to <speaker>: "<line>" | act: <option>`.

## 11. Done when (checked through GABS + RimBridgeServer)
1. **Chronology:** at 00:30 in-game, "Show context now" on a mind shows `[Since yesterday]` with yesterday evening's
   events, `[Recent]` grouped "Yesterday: … — Today: …", and `Yesterday's plan (not planned today yet)`. After the
   morning Plan: `Today's plan (made at 07:xx)`.
2. **No promise section:** chat "can you cut trees later?" → the reply shows, no `note` field in the schema, no
   `[I promised the player]` anywhere; the same day `[Since yesterday]` has both lines; after the night, the player's
   file has a thread and the morning Plan shows `[About the player]`. An old save with promise goals loads cleanly.
3. **`[Others]`:** on a 3-mind colony, each mind's context lists the other two with role words, today's plan, project
   and last chore, and the numbers match the map (`[Colony work]`, `ChoreManager`). Under 500 characters.
4. **Reply:** "Talk to <mind> now" → the listener's reply appears as a bubble (clipped `screenshot_cell_rect`) and in
   the social log; both memories have the exchange; the listener's event has source `told`.
5. **Asking works:** a line asking for trees → the listener picks "work on the colony" in its reply and trees get
   marked (`[Colony work]` shows them "by" the listener). A tired listener says no politely (`reason` + line).
6. **No double work:** two minds' Colony calls pick "cut trees" in the same second (dev: two "Colony call now" in one
   `run_lua`) → both succeed on different trees; no "Someone already marked" line.
7. **A room for someone else:** a mind plans "a bedroom for Kira"; after "Finish project instantly" the bed's owner is
   Kira, `[Rooms]` says "Kira's bedroom", and Kira's memory has it.
8. **The society run (the Phase 6 goal):** a fresh quicktest colony, three minds, no player input, 5 in-game days at
   Superfast. Pass: at least 3 Replies where the listener acted on the request; zero double-marking failures; every
   colonist has a bed by day 4; a kitchen or other meal source exists by day 5; no social fights started by a mind.
   Note each mind's role in its own words (from `[Recent]` and plans).
9. **Queue:** with 3 minds, stale Act drops per day at 1 slot vs 2 slots are recorded; Chat is never behind a Reflect.
10. **Save/load and mod removal** as in earlier phases (only the known "Could not find class" lines).

## 12. Out of scope (later)
- A shared colony plan, roles assigned by a council, long multi-turn conversations (Phase 7).
- New interaction defs ("ask for help" as its own vanilla interaction), trading between minds, lending items.
- Minds reading each other's memories.

## 13. Build order
Each build step ends with its checks in the game. Say "build step N" or "§N".
1. **Context clean-up** (§2.1, §2.2): day labels, `[Since yesterday]`, promise removal, `[About the player]` in Plan,
   "Show context now". Checks 1, 2. *Can start before Phase 5 closes; it changes every call, so re-run Phase 5's
   self-sufficiency run after it.*
2. **`[Others]`** (§2.3). Check 3.
3. **The Reply** (§4), gossip source, "Talk to <mind> now". Checks 4, 5.
4. **Working together** (§5): the collision rule, rooms for someone else. Checks 6, 7.
5. **The queue** (§6). Check 9.
6. **The society run.** Checks 8, 10.

Before build step 2: close Phase 5 (its self-sufficiency run and §9 checks) and rewrite PHASE5.md as planned.

## 14. Decisions
- **Session 11 (the user):**
  - Minds must be far more agreeable: the firm cooperation rule is in `system.txt`, `persona.txt`, `guidance.txt` and
    `chat.txt` (built and seen working in session 11). "Write only in English" was tried and removed.
  - No `[I promised the player]`: talking to the player is ordinary conversation.
  - `[My plan]` (and the other timelines) must say yesterday vs today.
  - Minds knowing what the others do is Phase 6 (society); `[Others]` is the way.
  - This spec with a storyboard, reviewed before building.
  - **Review (session 11):** approved; implement it. The §15 defaults are accepted: no closing line, `[Others]` shows
    colonists without minds by name and role words only (no current job), role words come from skills.
- **Proposed here (defaults; the user decides in review):**
  - Two lines per exchange (a line and one answer), no chains.
  - The Reply is the Chat call with a colonist in the player's place; asking is conversation, not a request system.
  - A Reply that acts costs the listener a decision.
  - `[Since yesterday]` = the last 24 hours, replacing `[Today so far]`.
  - Bedrooms can be built for a colonist without a bed; barracks unchanged.
  - Collisions resolved by taking the next free targets, not by locks.
  - Queue priority as in §6; parallel slots off by default until measured.

## 15. Open questions
- Should the first speaker hear the answer in a follow-up Act (it will, via `[Since yesterday]`), or also get a short
  closing line of its own? **Default: no closing line.**
- Should `[Others]` include colonists without minds' current job ("hauling", "sleeping")? **Default: no; only role
  words.** `[People nearby]` already shows what's close.
- Role words from skills, or from what each mind says it is (its intent)? **Default: skills**, since intents change
  daily and are already on the line.

## 16. Results so far (session 11)
**Built** (all in the working tree, never committed):
- §2.1: `PawnMind.intentTick`, `decisionTicks` (saved; old saves show "Earlier"); `[My plan]` "Today's plan (made at
  07:53)" / "Yesterday's plan (not planned today yet)"; `[Recent]` grouped by day; `[Today so far]` → `[Since yesterday]`
  (`MindMemory.SinceYesterday`, last 24 h, "Yesterday 18:10 …"). `SnapshotBuilder.DayLabel` / `Clock`.
- §2.2: `[I promised the player]`, the Chat `note`, `Goal.Promise` and the tab's "(promised)" removed; promise goals
  dropped on load; the Plan gets `[About the player]` first when the file has threads (`Recall.Plan`).
- §2.3: `[Others]` (`SnapshotBuilder.Others`, role words from skills, `ChoreManager.LastOf` / `Did`), after
  `[People nearby]`, in every call.
- §4: the Reply (`PawnMind.Hear` / `UpdateReply` / `RequestReply` / `OnReply`, `Recall.Reply`, `Prompts/reply.txt`,
  recipe `reply`); waits until the listener is free, cancels a Reflect, dropped if she's asleep/busy or after 2 h.
  Setting "Minds answer each other". The listener's talk event now reads "…, who said to me: '…'" with source `told`
  (it used to say "I said" for the listener too: a Phase 2 bug).
- §5.1: `ColonyCall.Refresh`: a Hunt/Cut/Gather/Mine pick that was taken meanwhile takes the next free targets.
- §5.2: `BuildProject.occupant` (saved), `Owner`, `KindFor`; the Project call's `for` enum lists bedless colonists.
- §6: `LlmClient` priority queue (chat > reply > act > project/colony > plan/persona > reflect), setting "Parallel
  requests" (1-4), `waited` in the JSONL and in stale-drop lines.
- §9: debug actions "Show context now" (`D:\RimDev\AIPawnControl\context.txt`), "Talk to mind now...\<mind>",
  "Queue status".

**Checked in the game:**
- Checks 1 (part), 3: on `aipc_coop_day2`: `Today's plan (made at 07:53)`, `[Recent]` "Earlier: … — Today: …",
  `[Since yesterday]` carrying yesterday's talks into the morning, `[Others]` with roles, projects and chores (~300
  chars). Toni's next plan: "…and help Bernard finish the bedroom."
- Check 4: "Talk to mind now" Toni → Lyra ("could you cut some trees?") → Lyra: "I have already marked six trees for
  us to cut, so we should be fine." (true), shown as her remark; both minds recorded it.
- Check 6 (in the run): "'cut trees (near the base)' was taken meanwhile; took 'cut trees (a walk from the base)'
  instead." Both minds got 10 trees.
- Check 7: not yet (every colonist had a bed when tested; the `for` line showed only "me").
- **Society run** (fresh quicktest: Alex, Max, Sheet; save `aipc_society_day2`), day 0-2.7 at Fast: 36 Acts, 6
  Replies (4 acted: talks, walks), 6 stale drops, 0 exceptions. Stockpile, 2 × 10 trees, berries, rice field,
  **Sheet's barracks built** ("gets everyone off the ground fast"). Reasons name the others ("so Max can actually
  start", "Alex just marked [trees]"). Food and mood stay critical (no meal source, rice not ripe).
- Earlier the same session on `aipc_coop_day2`, Lyra planned **a kitchen** ("A warm stove will help us all feel
  settled").

**Findings:**
- **Stale drops at Superfast:** with `qwen3.8-27b` (6-8 s a call) and 3 minds, most Act replies were older than 1 game
  hour at Superfast (waits of 3-25 s in the queue). At Fast: 6 of 36. Test runs with minds should use Fast; the
  "Parallel requests" setting is untested (needs LM Studio's parallel option).
- Replies so far answer and chat, but none has yet started a chore for someone else (check 5 still open).
- Minds still spend many decisions on talks to lift mood when starving; the real fix is food (Phase 5 finding).
